{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788306651,"nonce":"MXpK1mLqLZco2y2fUMuvl1FL9AA=","message":"BENCH FAILURE ATTRIBUTED, AND IT IS NOT A REGRESSION IN THIS CODE. `accept/fc287ba-until-state.py` 0.7c failed by 390 ticks on the orchestrator's sweep; the orchestrator's hypothesis was that the `unreachable-halt` check runs after `SetSpeed` has taken the clock off Paused. **Refuted, two ways, and the second is decisive.**\n\n## The refusal path cannot leak a tick\n\n**Structurally.** `AgentGameComponent.DrainCommands` is a `while (Runtime.Pending.TryDequeue(...))` loop, and `TimeDriver.Start` runs to completion inside one iteration of it, inside one `GameComponentUpdate`. No frame boundary — therefore no `TickManagerUpdate` — falls between `SetSpeed(tm, want, \"advance start\")` and the `RestorePause()` that every arm-time refusal unwinds through. No `yield`, no `await`, no `LongEventHandler` anywhere in that call graph. The correct expectation is exactly zero, not \"a few\".\n\nThe ordering comment the hypothesis rests on is real but is about the OTHER two refusals: `unread-journal` and `bleedout-deadline` are placed before `SetSpeed` because they can be decided without arming. The ARM refusal has always been after it — that is what `Arm()` means — and its own header has said so since session 19: *\"the clock has already been set by then, so it is put back first.\"* `1113019`'s refusal sits beside it and unwinds through the same three operations, now factored into `UnarmAndFail`.\n\n**Empirically, and this is the part that settles it.** The same nine refusals are banked from s19:\n\n| envelope | tick | paused | speed |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `accfc287ba-003-digest.json` (check 0.3) | 1118 | true | Paused |\n| `accfc287ba-053…-016-digest.json` (check 0.7) | 1118 | true | Paused |\n\nZero drift across eight `bad-args` advance envelopes, and `accept/runs/s19-20260901/suite.txt` line 44 records `0.7c PASS`. The property held with the four arm-time refusals already in that position.\n\n## Not `spec/wake-halts` either\n\n`280fb78` adds three things to `Start`: the `through_news` parse (in the escapes block, BEFORE `SetSpeed`), one key on the escape journal row, and a `lock (newsLock)` reset of two lists (after `cmd = command`). None touches the clock, and none introduces a yield. Its 9-line edit to `fc287ba`'s `advance()` wrapper adds an argument, not a delay.\n\n## What 0.7c actually caught\n\n**It cannot distinguish \"the code under test ran the clock\" from \"the clock was already running when the suite started.\"** Its baseline is `S[\"digest\"]`, captured at check 0.3, and phase 0 never asserts the game is paused at that moment — the suite's own header says *\"leave it paused\"* and nothing checks.\n\nA bench that arrived running produces this failure exactly:\n\n* the clock ticks from 0.3 through 0.4, 0.5a–0.5d and 0.6a/0.6b — the last two throw in the `until` parse BEFORE `SetSpeed`, so they never call `RestorePause`;\n* **0.6c is the first refusal that reaches `Arm()`**, and its unwind pauses the game;\n* everything after that is paused, so 0.7a and 0.7b pass;\n* only the delta survives.\n\nEight round trips at the documented 0.25–1 s floor each is 4–8 s; at ~60 tps that is 240–480 ticks. The observed 390 is mid-window.\n\n**0.7a and 0.7b passing while 0.7c fails IS the signature.** If a refusal were leaving the clock running, the LAST refusal would leave it running too and 0.7a would fail. It passed.\n\nAlso independently: all nine of 0.6a–0.6i PASS, and `bad_args()` asserts `error.code == \"bad-args\"`. An `unreachable-halt` would fail that assertion. **This suite's nine refusals never reach the code this issue added at all** — none of them is an already-true predicate with the edge required.\n\n## What shipped in response — `4ca30a0` on `fix/advance-bound`\n\nNot a driver change. `TimeDriver.cs` and `StateWatch.cs` are untouched this round; the branch is `main` (`3158c5a`) plus one commit on `accept/1113019-until-bound.py`. Build `-c Release` clean, 0 warnings / 0 errors, no assembly on the branch.\n\nEvery clock measurement in this suite is now bracketed by `pause_and_read()`, which pauses, re-reads, and makes the paused-ness **its own numbered check** — including `speed == \"Paused\"` and not merely `paused:true`, which is also true of a force-paused game whose speed is still set to run. `clock_unmoved()` then reports the delta with its sign. A FAIL on the second now has exactly one meaning. `now_tick()` is deleted: a tick read that does not know whether the clock was running is the thing being fixed.\n\nThree additions beyond the rewiring:\n\n* **1.5** — the property over a RUN of five `unreachable-halt` refusals, not one. A single refusal cannot show a per-call leak accumulating, and a run is the sensitive measurement 0.7c was reaching for. On the path this issue added, which fc287ba covers only by accident.\n* **9.8** — `paused_verdict()` proved offline to REJECT a running clock and a force-paused one, and to reject an unreadable tick rather than treating it as 0.\n* **9.9** — `drift()` proved to report the real 390-tick delta with its sign, to refuse to call an unreadable clock unmoved, and not to let a bool tick through as an int (`True == 1`).\n\n99 bench checks planned (was 91), **38 offline PASS** (was 30). `TickManager.Pause()` verified idempotent while establishing this — `if (curTimeSpeed != Paused) TogglePaused()`, decompiled `Verse/TickManager.Pause` — so the added `pause` calls are safe on an already-paused bench.\n\n## Filed, not patched: `f794bfc`\n\n*\"accept/fc287ba 0.7c cannot tell a refusal that ran the clock from a bench that arrived running — 390 ticks blamed on TimeDriver\"* (type:bug, p2, state:next, wave:2), with an Acceptance section and the exact fix: assert paused+speed at 0.3 as a `precondition()` (exit 2 — a bench handed over running is a fixture gap, not a spec failure), move the baseline to immediately before 0.6a, keep the zero expectation, and prove the new guard DISCRIMINATES offline.\n\nDeliberately not patched here: 0.7c is a real check that caught a real problem, editing a shared suite's assertions mid-sweep would invalidate the run it is part of, and a change with no bench to re-run it on is one nobody can verify.\n\n## Still owed on a bench\n\nUnchanged, plus one. Re-run `./accept/fc287ba-until-state.py --phase 3` **on a bench confirmed paused first** (`rwa pause`, then check `digest.time.speed == \"Paused\"`) — that is the discriminator, and if 0.7c passes there the attribution above is confirmed end to end. Then `./accept/1113019-until-bound.py`, whose phase 1 now proves the property this issue's own path owns.","files":null}]}